note Source: CPython Lib/dbm/dumb.py, _Database.iterkeys.
note After close(), self._index is set to None, so iter(None) raises TypeError, which is
note turned into dbm.dumb.error('DBM object has already been closed').

note WORKAROUND: the closed state is an index of None in Python. Here it is a maybe field.
note Example 20's `type Connection in state Closed or Open` would check this at compile time,
note but the original detects use-after-close at run time, so a run-time check is kept.
type Database
    field index as a maybe Map of Key to Position

note GAP: iterators - Python returns a lazy iterator over the live dict, which raises
note RuntimeError if the dict changes during iteration. The doc has no iterator or generator
note type; returning a List copies every key, which changes cost and loses the
note "changed during iteration" check.
note GAP: map type - _index is a dict from key to (offset, size). The doc has no Map; an
note invented `Map of Key to Position` and `keys of` are used.
to keys of index using memory
    given index is a Map of Key to Position
    given memory is an Allocator
    produces a List of Key
    fails with OutOfMemory
    uses memory

to iterkeys of database using memory
    given database is a Database
    given memory is an Allocator
    produces a List of Key
    fails with DatabaseClosed, OutOfMemory
    uses memory

    note WORKAROUND: Python catches the TypeError from iter(None) and re-raises a different
    note error `from None`, hiding the cause. In Scribe the closed case is tested directly, so
    note there is no exception to translate. The message text "DBM object has already been
    note closed" becomes the failure name DatabaseClosed, since failures carry no message.
    choose index of database
        when nothing
            fail with DatabaseClosed
        when some index
            give back try keys of index using memory
